# Example Terraform Variable Configurations

This file provides example configurations for different environments and use cases.

## Development Environment

```hcl
# terraform.tfvars
resource_group_name       = "rg-app-insights-dev"
application_insights_name = "ai-react-app-dev"
workbook_display_name     = "React App Dashboard - DEV"

dashboard_title       = "🔧 React App Insights - Development"
dashboard_description = "Development environment monitoring dashboard for debugging and testing."
time_range_days       = 7  # Shorter range for dev

# User Activity - More frequent checks in dev
user_activity_title         = "🔧 DEV - User Activity"
active_users_short_window   = "15m"
active_users_short_label    = "15 min"
active_users_short_minutes  = 15
login_activity_days         = 7
engagement_days             = 7

tags = {
  Environment = "development"
  Project     = "react-app-insights"
  CreatedBy   = "terraform"
}
```

## Production Environment

```hcl
# terraform.tfvars
resource_group_name       = "rg-app-insights-prod"
application_insights_name = "ai-react-app-prod"
workbook_display_name     = "React App Dashboard - PRODUCTION"

dashboard_title       = "📊 React App Insights - Production"
dashboard_description = "Production environment monitoring with performance tracking and error alerting."
time_range_days       = 90  # Longer range for trend analysis

# User Activity - Extended timeframes for production
user_activity_title       = "📊 PROD - User Activity"
login_activity_days       = 90
engagement_days           = 90
active_users_long_window  = "7d"
active_users_long_label   = "Last 7 Days"
active_users_long_hours   = 168

tags = {
  Environment = "production"
  Project     = "react-app-insights"
  CreatedBy   = "terraform"
  CostCenter  = "engineering"
}
```

## Staging Environment

```hcl
# terraform.tfvars
resource_group_name       = "rg-app-insights-staging"
application_insights_name = "ai-react-app-staging"
workbook_display_name     = "React App Dashboard - STAGING"

dashboard_title       = "🧪 React App Insights - Staging"
dashboard_description = "Pre-production testing environment with comprehensive monitoring."
time_range_days       = 30

tags = {
  Environment = "staging"
  Project     = "react-app-insights"
  CreatedBy   = "terraform"
}
```

## Custom Branding Example

```hcl
# terraform.tfvars
resource_group_name       = "rg-app-insights-prod"
application_insights_name = "ai-react-app-prod"
workbook_display_name     = "Acme Corp - Customer Portal"

dashboard_title       = "Acme Corp - Customer Portal Analytics"
dashboard_description = "Real-time monitoring and analytics for the Acme Corp customer-facing portal."
time_range_days       = 60

tags = {
  Environment = "production"
  Project     = "customer-portal"
  CreatedBy   = "terraform"
  Department  = "customer-success"
  Owner       = "platform-team@acme.com"
}
```

## Multi-Environment Deployment

You can create separate `.tfvars` files for each environment:

### File: `dev.tfvars`
```hcl
dashboard_title = "🔧 Development Dashboard"
time_range_days = 7
```

### File: `staging.tfvars`
```hcl
dashboard_title = "🧪 Staging Dashboard"
time_range_days = 30
```

### File: `prod.tfvars`
```hcl
dashboard_title = "📊 Production Dashboard"
time_range_days = 90
```

### Usage

```bash
# Deploy to development
terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"

# Deploy to staging
terraform apply -var-file="staging.tfvars"

# Deploy to production
terraform apply -var-file="prod.tfvars"
```

## Command Line Override

You can also override variables on the command line:

```bash
terraform apply \
  -var="dashboard_title=Custom Dashboard" \
  -var="time_range_days=45"
```

## Best Practices

1. **Environment-specific files**: Use separate `.tfvars` files for different environments
2. **Version control**: Commit `.tfvars.example` but add actual `.tfvars` to `.gitignore` if they contain sensitive values
3. **Consistent naming**: Use consistent naming conventions across environments
4. **Documentation**: Update variable descriptions when adding new template variables
5. **Testing**: Always run `terraform plan` before `terraform apply` to preview changes
